---
title: Configure & Regenerate Feature Flags
---

Now that you know how `VERA/Config/app-config.json` is structured, it's time to edit it and regenerate the Swift source that the app actually reads.

## Edit a Flag

Open `VERA/Config/app-config.json` and change one or more flags in `meetingRoomSettings`. For example, turn off screen sharing and switch the default layout to a grid:

```json
{
  "meetingRoomSettings": {
    "allowScreenShare": false,
    "defaultLayoutMode": "grid"
  }
}
```

Save the file once you've made your edit.

## Regenerate `AppConfig.swift`

From the `VERA/` directory, run the config generator script:

```sh
python3 ./Scripts/generate-app-config.py
```

The script reads `Config/app-config.json` and writes the generated Swift source to `VERAConfiguration/VERAConfiguration/AppConfig.swift`. On success, you'll see:

```
✅ Generated AppConfig.swift
```

| Script | Run from | Reads | Writes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `generate-app-config.py` | `VERA/` | `Config/app-config.json` | `VERAConfiguration/VERAConfiguration/AppConfig.swift` |

## Regenerate the Xcode Project

Feature flags are compiled into `AppConfig.swift`, so the Xcode project needs to be regenerated afterward:

```sh
tuist generate
```

{% aside type="caution" %}
If `app-config.json` has invalid JSON (a trailing comma, a missing quote, etc.), the generator script fails with something like:

```
❌ Error parsing JSON: Expecting ',' delimiter: line 12 column 3 (char 245)
```

Fix the JSON syntax in `VERA/Config/app-config.json` and re-run `python3 ./Scripts/generate-app-config.py` from `VERA/`.
{% /aside %}

Next, you'll move on to the app's theme tokens.
